---
title: 'The 70-point grading scale, and what separates MS-64 from MS-65'
date: 2026-11-03
description: 'How the 1 to 70 coin grading scale works, where it came from, and the marks, luster, strike and eye appeal that move a Mint State coin from MS-64 to MS-65.'
draft: false
---
Every graded coin carries a number, and for milled coins that number sits on a scale from 1 to 70. It is a compact way of saying how well a coin has been preserved, and because it is shared by collectors and dealers around the world, a grade means much the same thing wherever the coin is bought or sold. Here is how the scale works, and why a single point at the top of it can matter so much.

## Where the scale came from

The scale was proposed in 1949 by the American numismatist William Sheldon, originally for United States large cents. His idea was simple: give each state of preservation a number, so that coins could be compared without long written descriptions. A coin graded 1 was barely identifiable; a 70 was perfect. The descriptive names collectors already used, such as Good, Fine and Uncirculated, were mapped onto the numbers, and the scale was gradually extended to milled coinage generally.

Ancient coins are a different case. Because they were struck by hand, they are usually given a descriptive grade with separate assessments of strike and surface, rather than a number from 1 to 70. Our [grading standards](/grading-standards) explain both approaches.

## The scale in brief

The scale divides into two parts: circulated grades, which describe how much wear a coin has, and Mint State grades, for coins with no wear at all.

- **1 to 3 (Poor, Fair, About Good):** heavily worn, with the date or legends partly gone.
- **4 to 10 (Good, Very Good):** the main design is outlined, with some interior detail on the better examples.
- **12 to 35 (Fine, Very Fine):** moderate to light wear, with the major features sharp and finer details increasingly visible.
- **40 to 45 (Extremely Fine):** light wear on the highest points only.
- **50 to 58 (About Uncirculated):** just a trace of wear on the high points, often with much of the luster intact.
- **60 to 70 (Mint State):** no wear at all.

Proof coins, struck specially for collectors with mirrored or frosted surfaces, use the same numbers from 60 to 70 with a proof prefix.

Not every number is used in the circulated range. Grades cluster at familiar points such as 4, 8, 12, 20, 40, 45, 50, 55 and 58, because the differences between neighboring numbers would be too fine to judge consistently.

## Inside Mint State: wear is no longer the question

Below 60, a grade is mostly about wear: how much of the original detail is left. From 60 upward there is no wear to measure, so the grade is decided by four other qualities, judged together.

### Marks

Coins leave the press and immediately meet other coins: in bags, in counting machines, in rolls. The small nicks and scuffs this causes are called contact marks or bag marks. Their number, size, depth and, above all, their location are the largest single factor in most Mint State grades.

### Luster

Luster is the way light plays across the surface, produced by the flow lines left when the metal was struck. Full, unbroken luster that "cartwheels" around the coin as it is tilted lifts a grade; luster that is dull, patchy or interrupted by hairlines lowers it.

### Strike

A strong strike brings up every detail of the design, right down to the hair strands and the feathers. A weak strike leaves high points soft or flat, even though the coin has never been in circulation. Some types are known for being weakly struck, and graders take that into account, but a sharp strike is always an advantage.

### Eye appeal

The last factor is the overall impression: how the coin looks as a whole, including its color and toning. Attractive, original toning can help; blotchy or dark toning can hold a coin back. Eye appeal is the most subjective of the four, which is why it is weighed together with the others rather than on its own.

## MS-64 versus MS-65

The step from MS-64 to MS-65 is one of the most closely watched lines on the whole scale. MS-63 is often called "Choice", and MS-65 "Gem". MS-64 sits between them: a very attractive coin that falls just short of Gem.

In general terms, the difference comes down to these points:

- **Fewer and lighter marks.** An MS-64 coin may have several light marks or a few that are noticeable to the eye. An MS-65 coin has only minor marks, and none that draw attention at normal viewing distance.
- **Where the marks sit.** A mark in a focal area, such as the cheek or neck of a portrait or a large open field, counts for far more than the same mark hidden in the hair, the lettering or a busy part of the design. Two coins with the same number of marks can receive different grades for this reason alone.
- **Luster.** A Gem coin generally has strong, attractive luster with little interruption. Luster that is slightly subdued or broken in places can be enough to keep a coin at 64.
- **Strike and eye appeal.** A coin that is borderline on marks can be pushed up by a full strike and exceptional eye appeal, or held back by a soft strike or unattractive color.

No single factor decides it. A coin with almost no marks but dull luster may grade lower than one with a couple of tiny marks and outstanding luster. That is why grading is a matter of trained judgement applied to the whole coin, and why every grade we assign is reviewed again before the coin is sealed, as described in [our process](/process).

## Why one point can matter

For many series, there are far fewer coins in Gem condition and above than in the grades just below, and collectors building high-grade sets compete for them. That is why the market often treats MS-65 as a threshold, and why the difference in value between a 64 and a 65 can be much larger than the difference between, say, a 62 and a 63. How large it is depends entirely on the coin, its date and the market at the time.

## Higher still

Above Gem, MS-66 and MS-67 are coins with exceptional surfaces and only the smallest marks. MS-68 and MS-69 are close to flawless, with imperfections visible only under magnification. MS-70 describes a coin with no post-production imperfections visible under magnification at all.

If you have a coin you believe sits near one of these lines, the best preparation is to leave it exactly as it is. Our article on [how to spot a cleaned coin](/blog/how-to-spot-a-cleaned-coin) explains why, and when you are ready, you can [tell us what you would like to send](/submit).
